<h1>Getting Started</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Install Gate, run your first scan, and learn what happens when secrets are found.</p>

    <h2 id="installation">Installation</h2>

    <h3>Zero-install (npx)</h3>
    <p>The fastest way to try Gate. No installation required -- npx downloads and runs it in one step.</p>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">npx gate</span></div>

    <h3>Global install (npm)</h3>
    <p>For regular use, install globally so the <code>gate</code> command is always available.</p>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">npm install -g @penumbraforge/gate</span>

<span class="cmt"># Verify the installation</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate version</span>
<span class="dim">gate v2.0.0</span></div>

    <h3>Homebrew (coming soon)</h3>
    <p>A Homebrew formula is planned for future release.</p>
    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">brew install gate</span>  <span class="cmt"># coming soon</span></div>

    <h2 id="first-run">First Run</h2>

    <p>Run <code>npx gate</code> (or just <code>gate</code> if installed globally) in any directory. Gate scans all files in the current directory and its subdirectories for hardcoded secrets.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">npx @penumbraforge/gate</span>

<span class="ok">  ✓</span> <span class="dim">Scanned 847 files in 1.2s</span>

<span class="dim">  Gate v2.0.1 · 148 rules · scanning 847 files</span>

<span class="dim">  [1/1]</span> <span class="err">CRITICAL</span>  <span class="file">src/api/payments.ts:23</span>
<span class="dim">  rule:</span> stripe-secret-key

<span class="dim">   21 |</span> const stripe = require('stripe');
<span class="dim">   22 |</span>
<span class="dim">   23 |</span> const key = "<span class="underline err">sk_live_51Hb3azLk...R4pM</span>";
<span class="dim">   24 |</span> const client = stripe(key);

<span class="dim">  ──────────────────────────────────────</span>
<span class="dim">  1 finding in 1 file (1 critical) — 1.2s</span></div>

    <p>Gate automatically skips binary files, node_modules, .git directories, and other common non-source paths. No configuration needed.</p>

    <h2 id="project-setup">Project Setup</h2>

    <p>For ongoing use in a project, run <code>gate init</code> to create a configuration file and install the pre-commit hook.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate init</span>

<span class="ok">  Created .gaterc</span>
<span class="ok">  Installed pre-commit hook</span>
<span class="dim">  Gate will now scan staged files before every commit.</span></div>

    <p>This creates two things:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">.gaterc</strong> -- a YAML configuration file with sensible defaults. See <a href="/gate/wiki/configuration/">Configuration</a> for all options.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Pre-commit hook</strong> -- automatically scans staged files before each commit. If a secret is found, the commit is blocked until you fix, vault, or ignore the finding.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="how-scanning-works">How Scanning Works</h2>

    <p>Gate uses 148 detection rules to find secrets in your code. Each rule targets a specific provider or pattern type (AWS keys, GitHub tokens, database URLs, private keys, etc.).</p>

    <p>The scanning process:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Gate collects all files in the target directory (or staged files in pre-commit mode).</li>
      <li>Binary files, known non-source paths, and files matching <code>.gateignore</code> patterns are skipped.</li>
      <li>Each file is scanned against all 148 rules. Rules use regular expressions with contextual validation to reduce false positives.</li>
      <li>Matches are classified by severity: <span style="color: var(--error);">CRITICAL</span>, <span style="color: var(--warn);">HIGH</span>, <span style="color: var(--text-2);">MEDIUM</span>, <span style="color: var(--text-3);">LOW</span>.</li>
      <li>If <code>--verify</code> is enabled, Gate checks whether each credential is still active by making read-only API calls to the provider.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="when-a-secret-is-found">What Happens When a Secret Is Found</h2>

    <p>When Gate detects a secret, you have several options depending on the mode you are running in.</p>

    <h3>Default mode (non-interactive)</h3>
    <p>Gate prints all findings and exits with a non-zero exit code if any secrets were found. This is the mode used in CI and pre-commit hooks.</p>

    <h3>Interactive mode</h3>
    <p>When you run <code>gate scan --interactive</code> (or <code>gate</code> bare), Gate presents each finding with single-keypress options:</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="accent">  [f]</span><span class="dim"> fix</span>       Extract to .env and rewrite source code
<span class="accent">  [v]</span><span class="dim"> vault</span>     Encrypt and store in Gate vault
<span class="accent">  [i]</span><span class="dim"> ignore</span>    Mark as intentional (adds to .gateignore)
<span class="accent">  [s]</span><span class="dim"> skip</span>      Skip for now
<span class="accent">  [p]</span><span class="dim"> previous</span>  Go to previous finding
<span class="accent">  [j]</span><span class="dim"> jump</span>      Jump to finding by number
<span class="accent">  [?]</span><span class="dim"> explain</span>   Full remediation guide
<span class="accent">  [q]</span><span class="dim"> quit</span>      Exit with batch action on skipped</div>

    <h3>Pre-commit hook</h3>
    <p>When running as a pre-commit hook, Gate scans only staged files (<code>--staged</code> mode). If secrets are found, the commit is blocked. You must fix the finding (using <code>gate fix</code>), add an ignore comment, or update your <code>.gateignore</code> before the commit will succeed.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
      <strong>Tip:</strong> If Gate's auto-fix modifies your files during a pre-commit scan, it automatically re-stages the fixed files so your commit includes the remediated code.
    </div>

